Optimal Solution - Greedy solution that merges overlapping intervals after sorting by start time.
                   Sort intervals by start time, then merge consecutively overlapping ones in a single pass.

class Solution {
    public int[][] merge(int[][] intervals) {
        // If there are 0 or 1 intervals, no merging is needed
        if (intervals.length <= 1)
            return intervals;
        // Sort intervals based on starting time
        Arrays.sort(intervals, Comparator.comparingInt(i -> i[0]));
        // List to store merged intervals
        List<int[]> res = new ArrayList<>();
        // Initialize with the first interval
        int[] newInterval = intervals[0];
        res.add(newInterval);
        // Traverse all intervals
        for (int[] interval : intervals) {
            // If current interval overlaps with the last merged interval
            if (interval[0] <= newInterval[1]) {
                // Merge by extending the end time
                newInterval[1] = Math.max(newInterval[1], interval[1]);
            } 
            // If no overlap, start a new merged interval
            else {
                newInterval = interval;
                res.add(newInterval);
            }
        }
        // Convert list to array and return
        return res.toArray(new int[res.size()][]);
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(N log N).
Space Complexity :- O(N).
